| Expenses           | 0     | 45.00  | true       |
| Expenses:Groceries | 1     | 45.00  | false      |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A category that holds both transactions and other categories
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule A category with its own transactions shows them on an Other row
Description A category that contains other categories can still be chosen for a
# transaction. Expenses:Auto has Fuel and Insurance under it and also
# has petrol bought before anyone made a Fuel category, and cash spent
# on the car that belongs nowhere more precise.
#
# The transaction is associated with the containing category, exactly as
# chosen. The report is where it shows differently: the containing
# category carries the total of everything beneath it, and its own
# transactions appear as a row under it called Other, so that the rows
# underneath add up to the row above.
Details Without that row the arithmetic of the report is wrong in a way that is
# hard to see: Auto reads 100,948.56, its children add to 99,862.97, and
# the missing 1,085.59 is money posted to Auto itself with nothing on the
# page to say so. A reader checking the column would find the error and
# have no way to explain it.
#
# The row is not a category. Nobody chooses it, nothing is posted to it,
# and it is not in the chart of accounts. It exists for the length of the
# report, which is why choosing the containing category is the whole of
# what a person does.
#
# The name follows Quicken, because the reports are checked against
# Quicken's: the word Other, then the containing category's path with the
# section name taken off. So Expenses:Auto gives "Other Auto", and
# Expenses:Cash Expenditures:Foreign Travel gives "Other Cash
# Expenditures:Foreign Travel".
Examples: OtherRowNaming
| ContainingCategory                         | TheRowIsCalled                         |
| Expenses:Auto                              | Other Auto                             |
| Expenses:Cash Expenditures                 | Other Cash Expenditures                |
| Expenses:Cash Expenditures:Foreign Travel  | Other Cash Expenditures:Foreign Travel |
| Income:Div Income                          | Other Div Income                       |
| Income:Tax Refund                          | Other Tax Refund                       |

Attributes OtherRowNaming
| Attribute          | Type        | Default |
| ContainingCategory | AccountPath |         |
| TheRowIsCalled     | Text        |         |

Scenario A category with its own transactions and children shows an Other row
Description Forty on petrol under Fuel, ten on something to do with the car
# posted to Auto itself. Auto totals fifty, and the two rows beneath it
# add to fifty.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Auto          | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | Expense | false       |
Given dated entries are : DatedAccountEntry
| Date       | Account            | Amount |
| 2026-03-01 | Assets:Checking    | -40.00 |
| 2026-03-01 | Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 40.00  |
| 2026-03-02 | Assets:Checking    | -10.00 |
| 2026-03-02 | Expenses:Auto      | 10.00  |
When report run : ReportSpec Vertical
| GroupBy | Category |
| From | 2026-01-01 |
| To   | 2026-12-31 |
Then report rows are : ReportRow
| Account            | Amount | Level | IsSubtotal |
| Expenses           | 50.00  | 0     | true       |
| Expenses:Auto      | 50.00  | 1     | true       |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 40.00  | 2     | false      |
| Other Auto         | 10.00  | 2     | false      |
# Auto is the total of everything in it. Beneath it, Fuel's forty and its own
# ten, which add to the fifty above. The Other row comes last, after the real
# categories, which is where Quicken puts it -- and these reports are checked
# against Quicken's.

